<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Adaptive launcher icon.

  Two of the three layers come from the supplied artwork:
    background  - a flat colour lifting the dark navy out of the source PNG
    foreground  - the neon artwork, shipped as ic_launcher_foreground.png in
                  every density rather than as a vector
  The third does not, and cannot:
    monochrome   - ic_launcher_monochrome.png, the same mark reduced to a white
                  alpha silhouette at 62% of the layer

  The foreground is the source PNG at full bleed, 108dp. That is deliberate and
  it is what makes the icon look the same on every launcher instead of being
  cropped differently by each mask: the design runs to the edge of the canvas,
  so there is no artwork sitting outside the 18dp that a circular or squircle
  mask discards. Anything inset here would show up as a gap on some launchers
  and not on others, which is the problem this icon was meant to fix.

  The monochrome layer is a separate file rather than a second reference to the
  colour foreground. Android 13 tints a themed icon with one colour and uses the
  layer's alpha as the mask, and the supplied PNG has no alpha at all: it is a
  flat RGB image in which the dark navy field is as opaque as the neon strokes.
  Pointing monochrome at it therefore produced a themed icon that was a solid
  filled square, because every pixel had alpha 255. The foreground stays full
  bleed for the colour icon and only the mask needs transparency.

  The background colour is the exact navy sampled from the PNG (RGB 5,9,26).
  It has to be exact: the foreground is that same artwork at full bleed, so any
  mismatch shows as a seam around the edge on every launcher that draws the
  background layer at all.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>